IMPORT prelude

-- Passports Act 2007 (Singapore) -- how long a passport or temporary travel document lasts and whether it may be extended (ss 8, 9,
-- 14, 15), the Controller's grounds for refusal that turn on a past conviction, repeated losses, a concurrent passport or a child
-- (ss 19(2)(c)(ii), 20(2)(c)(ii), 25, 26, 27), the bar on acting on a withdrawn request (s 24(4)), the appeal to the Minister (s 28),
-- the holder's duties to report a loss and surrender on losing citizenship (ss 45, 46), the penalties for the main offences
-- (ss 29, 30, 36, 37, 39, 41, 42, 43, 44, 49, 56), and composition (s 52).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/PA2007.txt. The deposit's
-- cover says it "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021", but the body carries later annotations, the
-- latest being Act 16 of 2026 (wef 30/09/2026, s 47A, cross-border railway designated areas). The arrangement of sections at the top
-- of the deposit is out of step with the body (and some marginal headings sit one section late); the BODY's numbers are used.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 8 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the rules a holder, applicant or
-- parent meets. Not encoded: the general discretion to refuse or cancel (ss 19(1), 20(1), 21(1)), cancellation grounds in s 21(2)
-- and s 22, law-enforcement and harmful-conduct grounds beyond s 24(4), emergency and other travel documents (ss 16 to 18),
-- enforcement powers (ss 31 to 33), the false foreign travel document offences (ss 47, 47A), unauthorised issue (s 48), body
-- corporate liability (s 50), Part 6 beyond the s 56 penalty, and the regulations (prescribed offences, fees, competent authorities,
-- compoundable offences), none of which was retrieved.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Passports Act 2007 -- passports`

DECLARE Document IS ONE OF
    `a Singapore ordinary passport`
    `a Singapore diplomatic or official passport`
    `a Singapore temporary travel document`
    `a Singapore document of identity`

§§ `8, 9, 14, 15 -- validity and extension`

-- s 8(1): every Singapore ordinary passport "is valid for a period not exceeding 6 years" for those issued on or after 1 April 2005;
-- (2) a diplomatic or official passport is valid "for the period specified in the passport"; (3) the Minister may direct a shorter
-- period in a particular case; (4) the Minister may by Gazette notification increase the period [S 759/2022].
-- s 14(1): a Singapore temporary travel document "is valid for one year" (same shorter / longer powers in (2), (3)).
-- s 9: "Singapore passports that are biometric passports must not be extended."
-- s 15: "Singapore temporary travel documents must not be extended."
-- s 17: a document of identity is valid for as long as the Controller thinks sufficient for the journey. The Act states no bar on
-- extending a non-biometric passport or a document of identity (the encoding says only what the Act forbids).
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the longest ordinary passport validity in years` MEANS 6

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the temporary travel document validity in years` MEANS 1

GIVEN `the document` IS A Document, `it is biometric` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act forbids extending it` IF
    CONSIDER `the document`
    WHEN `a Singapore temporary travel document` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Singapore document of identity` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `it is biometric`

§§ `19(2)(c)(ii), 20(2)(c)(ii) -- refusal for a past conviction`

-- s 19(2)(c)(ii): the Controller may refuse a Singapore PASSPORT if he believes on reasonable grounds that the citizen "has, in the
-- 10 years before the application ... been convicted of an offence under this Act or the repealed Act, or of an arrestable offence
-- against any written law that is prescribed".
-- s 20(2)(c)(ii): the same for a Singapore TRAVEL DOCUMENT, but "in the 3 years before the application".
-- The conviction must be under this Act, the repealed Act, or a PRESCRIBED arrestable offence (the list is in regulations, not read).
GIVEN `it is a passport application` IS A BOOLEAN, `years between conviction and application` IS A NUMBER, `the conviction is of a qualifying offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conviction is a ground to refuse` IF
        `the conviction is of a qualifying offence`
    AND IF `it is a passport application`
        THEN `years between conviction and application` LESS THAN 10
        ELSE `years between conviction and application` LESS THAN 3

§§ `24(4) -- a withdrawn request`

-- s 24(3): on a competent authority's written request the Controller MAY cancel or refuse; (4) "Despite subsection (3)" the
-- Controller "must not" cancel or refuse "if that request ... has been withdrawn by the competent authority or is no longer
-- considered by the Controller to be current". (2): a request may be made whether or not the person has applied, holds a document,
-- or "is a citizen of Singapore".
GIVEN `the request was withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Controller considers the request current` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Controller may act on the request` IF
    (NOT `the request was withdrawn`) AND `the Controller considers the request current`

§§ `25 -- repeated loss`

-- s 25: the Controller may refuse a Singapore passport if (a) before the application "2 or more Singapore passports issued to the
-- person have been lost or stolen"; AND (b) "for reasons of passport security and integrity" the Controller considers it not
-- desirable to issue another.
GIVEN `passports lost or stolen before` IS A NUMBER, `the Controller considers another undesirable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `repeated loss permits refusal` IF
    `passports lost or stolen before` AT LEAST 2 AND `the Controller considers another undesirable`

§§ `26, 27 and 2 -- a concurrent passport; children`

-- s 26(1): the Controller "must not issue a Singapore passport to a person if the person has already been issued with a Singapore
-- passport or a Singapore travel document and that document is still valid"; (2) unless the Minister's directions under s 5(2)
-- provide otherwise.
-- s 2(1): "child" means "a person who is below 16 years of age".
-- s 27(1): the Controller "must not issue a Singapore passport or a Singapore travel document to a child unless" (a) a person with
-- parental responsibility (or, for a child in care, the Director-General of Social Welfare, a protector or the care-giver)
-- consents to the child travelling internationally, or (b) a court order permits it. s 2(4): parental responsibility = parent, a
-- person the child lives with under a court order, or a person entitled to guardianship or custody under written law.
GIVEN `the applicant's age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the applicant is a child` IF `the applicant's age` LESS THAN 16

GIVEN `the applicant's age` IS A NUMBER, `holds a valid passport or travel document` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Minister's directions allow it` IS A BOOLEAN, `the required person consents` IS A BOOLEAN, `a court order permits travel` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Controller must not issue the passport` IF
        (`holds a valid passport or travel document` AND (NOT `the Minister's directions allow it`))
    OR  (`the applicant is a child` `the applicant's age` AND (NOT `the required person consents`) AND (NOT `a court order permits travel`))

§§ `28 -- appeal to the Minister`

-- s 28(1): a person aggrieved by the Controller's decision (a) to refuse to issue, (b) to refuse to extend, or (c) to cancel a
-- passport or travel document, "other than a decision made because of a warrant of arrest issued in Singapore against the person",
-- may "within 14 days after being notified of the decision (or such longer period as the Minister allows in exceptional
-- circumstances, whether before or after the end of the 14 days)" appeal to the Minister, "whose decision is final". (2) in writing,
-- with grounds. s 28A: the Minister may designate a Second Minister, Minister of State or Parliamentary Secretary to hear it.
DECLARE Decision IS ONE OF
    `a refusal to issue`
    `a refusal to extend`
    `a cancellation`
    `an endorsement`

GIVEN `the decision` IS A Decision
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the decision is of an appealable kind` IF
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `an endorsement` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the decision` IS A Decision, `made because of a Singapore arrest warrant` IS A BOOLEAN, `days after notification` IS A NUMBER, `the Minister allowed longer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an appeal lies to the Minister` IF
        `the decision is of an appealable kind` `the decision`
    AND (NOT `made because of a Singapore arrest warrant`)
    AND (`days after notification` AT MOST 14 OR `the Minister allowed longer`)

§§ `45, 46 -- reporting a loss; surrender on ceasing to be a citizen`

-- s 45(1): the holder commits an offence if the document "is lost, stolen or destroyed", the holder knows it, and fails to report
-- it "to an authorised officer within 14 days after the first time the person knows"; (2) for a CHILD holder the offence is that of
-- "any person who has parental responsibility"; (3) $3,000 or 2 years; (4) not if the person "has a reasonable excuse".
-- s 46(1): a holder of a passport or temporary travel document who ceases to be a citizen, knows it, and fails to surrender the
-- document "within 24 hours after the first time the person knows"; (2) parent for a child; (3) reasonable excuse; $3,000 or 2 years.
GIVEN `days from knowing to reporting` IS A NUMBER, `has a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `failing to report the loss is an offence` IF
    `days from knowing to reporting` GREATER THAN 14 AND (NOT `has a reasonable excuse`)

GIVEN `hours from knowing to surrender` IS A NUMBER, `has a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `failing to surrender is an offence` IF
    `hours from knowing to surrender` GREATER THAN 24 AND (NOT `has a reasonable excuse`)

GIVEN `the holder's age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the parent bears the reporting duty` IF `the holder's age` LESS THAN 16

§§ `29, 30, 36, 37, 39, 41 to 44, 49, 56 -- penalties`

-- Maxima as printed (fine in dollars; imprisonment converted to months):
--   s 29(2), 30(2) failing immediately to surrender on demand -- $5,000 or 6 months
--   s 36(1) making a false passport to dishonestly induce acceptance -- $10,000 or 10 years
--   s 37(1) possessing a false passport, knowing -- $10,000 or 10 years
--   s 39(1) knowingly false or misleading statement in an application -- $10,000 or 10 years
--   s 41(1) using a cancelled passport -- $10,000 or 10 years
--   s 41(3) giving one's own passport to another, reckless as to its use -- $5,000 or 3 years
--   s 42(1) selling a passport -- $10,000 or 10 years
--   s 42(2) "engages in the business or trade of selling" -- "imprisonment for a presumptive minimum term of not less than 2 years
--           but not more than 15 years and shall also be punished with a fine not exceeding $20,000" [15/2019]
--   s 43 damaging or destroying without reasonable excuse -- $3,000 or 2 years
--   s 44(1) buying or offering to buy -- $10,000 or 10 years
--   s 45 failing to report a loss -- $3,000 or 2 years
--   s 49 obstructing an officer -- $5,000 or 2 years
--   s 56(1) unauthorised access to identifying information -- $5,000 or 3 years
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `failing to surrender on demand`
    `making a false passport`
    `possessing a false passport`
    `a false statement in an application`
    `using a cancelled passport`
    `lending one's own passport recklessly`
    `selling a passport`
    `the business of selling passports`
    `damaging a passport`
    `buying a passport`
    `failing to report a loss`
    `obstructing an officer`
    `unauthorised access to identifying information`

GIVEN `the offence` IS A Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `failing to surrender on demand` THEN 5000
    WHEN `lending one's own passport recklessly` THEN 5000
    WHEN `the business of selling passports` THEN 20000
    WHEN `damaging a passport` THEN 3000
    WHEN `failing to report a loss` THEN 3000
    WHEN `obstructing an officer` THEN 5000
    WHEN `unauthorised access to identifying information` THEN 5000
    OTHERWISE 10000

GIVEN `the offence` IS A Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `failing to surrender on demand` THEN 6
    WHEN `lending one's own passport recklessly` THEN 36
    WHEN `the business of selling passports` THEN 180
    WHEN `damaging a passport` THEN 24
    WHEN `failing to report a loss` THEN 24
    WHEN `obstructing an officer` THEN 24
    WHEN `unauthorised access to identifying information` THEN 36
    OTHERWISE 120

GIVEN `the offence` IS A Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the presumptive minimum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `the business of selling passports` THEN 24
    OTHERWISE 0

§§ `52 -- composition`

-- s 52(1): the Controller "may compound any offence under this Act that may be prescribed as a compoundable offence by collecting
-- ... a sum not exceeding $500"; (2) on payment "no further proceedings are to be taken". Which offences are prescribed is in the
-- regulations (not read).
GIVEN `the offence is prescribed as compoundable` IS A BOOLEAN, `the sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence may be compounded for the sum` IF
    `the offence is prescribed as compoundable` AND `the sum` AT MOST 500
